Reward of 50,000 yuan Hubei Enshi praised the sand dam landslide "early warning first person"-Zhongxin.com
Hubei Daily News (Reporter Lin Jing) On July 26, Enshi City notified Chen Guangming, a geological disaster site monitor who made an important contribution to the early warning of sand dam landslides, and sent him a bonus of 50,000 yuan.
At the same time, party members and cadres in the city are called upon to learn his spirit of dedication, courage, initiative and dedication.
On July 21st, a landslide occurred in the sand dam of the horse village in Tunbao Township, Enshi City, resulting in the formation of the dry channel of the Qing River, and 58-year-old Chen Guangming was the first person to warn about the landslide.
On the afternoon of July 17, Chen Guangming learned the news of the cracking of the walls in the villagers' homes, and immediately went to the scene to view it.
He had just walked out of his home for five or six hundred meters, so he saw that the village road cracked four or five centimeters, along the road all the way to the cornfield, the length of about 50 meters.
Chen was alert, and the photos of the cracking of the highway were sent to the "geological disaster monitor contact group" in the township for the first time.
The relevant person in charge of the Tuinbao Township Natural Planning Institute reviewed and judged on the spot, and the possibility of the landslide was very large, and the decision to evacuate the masses was immediately made.
Chen Guangming's timely early warning saved 36 hours of valuable time for the safe evacuation of 1339 people from 315 households in the affected area.
In this major geological disaster, there were no casualties.
Chen Guangming's serious and responsible professionalism was praised by the masses.
In the face of praise, he said, "There is no one who was saved, it is my responsibility."
Australian University develops new drug is expected to be used to treat blood vessel blockages caused by the new crown - Xinhua
SYDNEY, July 26 (Xinhua) -- Researchers at Monash University in Australia are working on a new drug that will help prevent heart disease and stroke, and hopes that it can be used to treat blood vessel blockages caused by the new coronavirus.
The researchers found that an enzyme in platelets accelerated platelet coagulation before a heart disease or stroke occurred.
Therefore, they developed a drug that can temporarily inhibit platelet coagulation, so as to avoid blocked blood vessels and play a preventive role in heart disease and stroke.
According to the researchers, the drug only inhibits the clotting effect of platelets when signs of heart disease or stroke occur.
Since studies have shown that similar vascular blockages are one of the leading causes of death in patients with new coronary diseases, the researchers hope that this drug can be used for the treatment of such severe patients in the future.
At present, the drug has achieved good results in animal model experiments, and the researchers are preparing for the next phase of human clinical trials.
The results of the study have recently been published in the United States "science and translational medicine" magazine.
